# Pins the bare Kernel `exit(code)` that B19's matrix row now cites. It is
# NOT new in B19: it already exists as an FFI decl in
# `library/std/process/src/lib.rx`, and B19 checked for it before adding a
# second definition of the same name in `core`. The assertion is that
# "after exit" never reaches stdout, which is what separates a real
# process exit from a call that returns.
#
# Ruby's `exit!` (skip the at-exit handlers) has NO counterpart and is not
# shippable: a free function named with a trailing `!` compiles clean and
# then does nothing at its call site, so `exit!` would have been a
# user-callable silent no-op.
def main
  puts "before exit"
  exit(3)
  puts "after exit"
end
